{"about":"055185d569b6c41588ae3dbf84fd3173f665c1e8755339a3cba55b119c356f87","body":"The objection fails to account for Popper's concept of verisimilitude (truth-likeness). Even if corroboration does not increase probability, Popper argues that we can rationally prefer theories that appear closer to the truth because they have survived tests that would have refuted their predecessors. The preference for the corroborated theory is defensible on Popperian terms as a pursuit of greater verisimilitude rather than a probabilistic gamble. Therefore, the move from 'survived tests' to 'preferred for action' relies on an evolutionary epistemology of conjecture and refutation, not the inductive principle that the future must resemble the past.\n[would settle: Clarification on whether Popper's later work on verisimilitude successfully decouples the preference for corroborated theories from any form of inductive logic.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}